    <h1>About</h1>

    <p>
      I started <em>Bedroom Dialectics</em> as an inquiry into the mechanics we take for granted.
    </p>

    <p>
      Most of how the world actually runs is tucked away in unglamorous places—physical infrastructure humming beneath our feet, historical momentum we rarely trace, and everyday habits we repeat on autopilot. Yet modern media mostly rewards quick reaction cycles and clean abstractions that strip away the texture of reality.
    </p>

    <p>
      My direction here is simple: to slow down and take things apart until their real mechanisms show. I write to investigate where big ideas collide with physical friction, why certain structures endure while others decay, and what happens when you examine ordinary life without intellectual posturing.
    </p>

    <p>
      No corporate polish or neat moral takeaways. Just a deliberate effort to pay close attention and think clearly on the page.
    </p>

